WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
The multi-billion dollar artificial
intelligence industry is trapped in a

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.680
paradox, spending fortunes trying to teach
unpredictable machines to behave.

3
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.960
The default method for securing a large
language model is

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.960
called behavioral alignment.

5
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.840
This is the attempt to train models into
compliance with

6
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.800
a specific policy through instruction.

7
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.840
This diagram reveals the flaw in that
approach.

8
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.360
The space of possible inputs is
mathematically too large to

9
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.360
patch, meaning a single filtration node
will eventually be overwhelmed

10
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.440
by adversarial prompts.

11
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.480
This is the philosophy of coercive design.

12
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.560
It is a system built on the demand that a

13
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.320
flawed component must comply with the
rules set for it.

14
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.960
This approach relies on a category error.

15
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.000
It treats the language model as if it
possesses deliberative

16
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.360
agency, as if the system has the capacity
to consciously

17
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.440
decide to follow instructions.

18
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.440
When we try to make an inherently
unpredictable system more

19
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.840
aligned, we are just adding layers of
complicity to a

20
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.240
core that remains unreliable.

21
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.600
Safety that depends on the AI deciding to
be good

22
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.280
creates a structural vulnerability in the
system's design.

23
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.160
To find a different blueprint, we have to
look outside

24
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.840
of server farms and into a domestic
setting where practitioners

25
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.880
have dealt with unreliable components for
decades.

26
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.960
Pathological demand avoidance is a profile
where a perceived directive

27
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.000
triggers an immediate, involuntary
refusal.

28
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.440
It is a fast, low-level pattern match that
happens before

29
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.680
the brain can process the instruction.

30
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.720
While the biology and the code are
different, the design

31
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.080
challenge is the same.

32
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.000
When a component's response is predictably
triggered by certain inputs,

33
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.640
the solution has to come from the system
built around

34
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.480
it. This was the motivation for Spark, a
Raspberry Pi-based

35
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.760
robot companion designed for a
neurodivergent child.

36
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.720
The central principle of its design is
that the robot

37
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:54.840
is physically incapable.

38
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.680
of giving orders Spark uses declarative
first language.

39
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.360
If a child needs shoes, it only observes,
the shoes

40
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.480
are by the door.

41
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.000
It routes all interaction through a
pattern that makes imperatives

42
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.160
off-limits. This structure creates a
bounded action surface.

43
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.880
The model at the center can generate an
order, but

44
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.920
the architecture around it refuses to pass
that command to

45
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.680
the speaker. The SPARC robot demonstrates
that a safe outcome

46
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.280
can be achieved, even with an
unpredictable core, by making

47
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.320
the surrounding structure the
authoritative gatekeeper.

48
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.280
For this to work at scale, the
architecture must be

49
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.480
able to lock down completely during a
crisis, rather than

50
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.800
trying to reason its way through a
failure.

51
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.480
This concentric circle model shows how
Spark handles a meltdown.

52
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.160
When a specific signature is detected, the
system bypasses the

53
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.400
model entirely and enters a deterministic
quiet mode, governed by

54
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.000
these overriding logic gates.

55
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.400
You see the same shape in therapeutic AI.

56
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.440
When a user is in crisis, the system
doesn't trust

57
00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.960
the model's discretion.

58
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.960
It uses these parallel processing channels
to trigger pre-validated grounding

59
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:07.000
scripts instead. The third ring represents
sideways engagement, treating the

60
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.880
communication channel itself as a safety
feature.

61
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.720
By narrating facts or puzzles, the
architecture routes around the

62
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.440
refusal response before it can be
triggered.

63
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.240
Finally, there is the basic e-speak
fallback.

64
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.360
If the high-level cloud servers or the
local AI models

65
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.960
crash, the system defaults to a basic
immutable voice engine

66
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.960
to ensure it never goes mute.

67
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.160
Spark is a blueprint for non-coercive
design.

68
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.720
It shifts the load of safety from the
behavior of

69
00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.680
the component to the structure of the
environment.

70
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.960
These domestic lessons provide a roadmap
for how we deploy

71
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.600
artificial intelligence at an enterprise
level.

72
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.400
True AI safety is a property of the
deployment architecture,

73
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.760
not a behavioral trait programmed into the
model itself.

74
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.680
We can measure this using the SPARC test.

75
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.880
It's a simple diagnostic for any AI system
currently being

76
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.120
safety review by regulators or boards.

77
00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.520
Strip the model out of the deployment
entirely.

78
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.640
If the remaining architecture still
enforces the safe and desired

79
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.520
outcome by default, the system is
non-coercive.

80
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.840
If the safety of a system rests entirely
on a

81
00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.040
model's training, it remains vulnerable to
the infinite variety of

82
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.600
inputs that training can never fully
cover.

83
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.040
We cannot reason with an inherently
unpredictable substrate.

84
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.960
We can only build the room around it.

85
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:20.880
The shoes are by the door.

